To port some code from AIX, we wish to use 32-bit pointers. The
C compiler appears to provide the -xtaso_short flag for this.
We have found several problems with it. One is that it does not
seem to be consistant. Here is the output of a simple program to
print the size of objects. Note that witht he -xtaso_short flag,
sizeof(int*) is 4 while sizeof(&i) where i is an integer is still 8.

=================================================================

$ cc -g -taso -xtaso_short ptest.c -o ptest
$ ./ptest
Sizeof(int)     = 4
Sizeof(int*)    = 4
Sizeof(long)    = 8
Sizeof(long*)   = 4
Sizeof(i)       = 4
Sizeof(&i)      = 8
Sizeof(l)       = 8
Sizeof(&l)      = 8

$ cc -g ptest.c -o ptest
$ ./ptest
Sizeof(int)     = 4
Sizeof(int*)    = 8
Sizeof(long)    = 8
Sizeof(long*)   = 8
Sizeof(i)       = 4
Sizeof(&i)      = 8
Sizeof(l)       = 8
Sizeof(&l)      = 8

$ cat ptest.c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <strings.h>

int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
  int i;
  long l;

  printf("Sizeof(int)     = %d\n", sizeof(int));
  printf("Sizeof(int*)    = %d\n", sizeof(int*));
  printf("Sizeof(long)    = %d\n", sizeof(long));
  printf("Sizeof(long*)   = %d\n", sizeof(long*));
  printf("Sizeof(i)       = %d\n", sizeof(i));
  printf("Sizeof(&i)      = %d\n", sizeof(&i));
  printf("Sizeof(l)       = %d\n", sizeof(l));
  printf("Sizeof(&l)      = %d\n", sizeof(&l));
  
  exit(0);
}

=================================================================

The other problems is that the argv argument to main is still 64
bytes and if you pass argv to another function as (char **) and
try to access it with string functions, it core dumps.  The simple
work around here was to copy the entire argv contents in main to
a local (char **) variable using repeated mallocs.

A similar problem to this is that many functions core-dump in
32-mode. One of these is tolower. The following code coredumps.

=================================================================

$ cat xtest.c
#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <ctype.h>

void cUtToUpper(const char* is,char* os){
  int i, l=strlen(is);
  for(i=0; i<l; i++) os[i]=toupper(is[i]);
  os[i]=0;
}

main(int argc, char **argv)
{
  char s1[100];

  strcpy(s1,"event");

  cUtToUpper(s1,s1);
  
  printf("%s\n",s1);

}

$ cc -g xtest.c -o xtest
$ ./xtest
EVENT
$ cc -g -taso -xtaso_short xtest.c -o xtest
$ ./xtest
Segmentation fault (core dumped)
$ dbx xtest core
dbx version 3.11.8
Type 'help' for help.
Core file created by program "xtest"

signal Segmentation fault at   [cUtToUpper:8 +0x4c,0x12001310]  \
   for(i=0; i<l; i++) os[i]=toupper(is[i]);

=================================================================





