In article <4fqe24$4b3@news.bu.edu>, vicky@bu.edu (George Papadimitriou) writes:
> 
> *Which chips are responsible for the SCC?
> ========================================================================
> UC80234Q GD | 				ITA27 B3 |
> DU 9347	    |2 (1 pair)			ST 93B401|2 (1 pair)
> SR 34645    |				MALAYSIA |
> 
> Fil-Mag  |				Fil-Mag |
> 23Z109SM |4 (2 pairs)			106 SM  |4 (2 pairs)
> TWN 9417 |				TWN 9421|
> ========================================================================

The Fil-Mags are line filters. I think the other two are the line drivers
and receivers, but I'm not sure (the part numbers don't ring a bell, but
they would be connected to the Fil-Mags and pretty close to them). The SCCs
themselves are probably made by Zilog and stamped with a part number
beginning with Z and having an 8530 in it. My Zilog data books list likely
part numbers as Z85C3010.

Data flows between the SCCs and the line drivers and receivers, which 
convert the data to RS232 levels. From the line drivers and receivers, the
data flows through the Fil-Mags because the machines have to meet FCC
regs. There should also be static protection, which would look like a
bunch of miscellaneous small surface mount parts.
