kunkee@Starbase.NeoSoft.COM (Randy Kunkee) writes:

>Actually, as I read RFCs 821 and 822, the destination mailbox (which is
>not required to be a user) is to be preserved for final interpretation
>by the destination system.  In  other words, SMTP software must not
>mess with the case of the mailbox (usually a user ID).  The remaining
>portion (system, domain name, etc) is insensitive to case
>as you say.  I can quote lines from the RFCs on this.

Now you make all people who may send you mail read, understand and
live by RFC822.  Done that?, OK now make the people who implement
other mail systems do the same - and fix their software.

You cannot rely on case getting through email unscathed - VAX/VMS
systems for example tend to upcase *everything*.


>I like one other guy's answer, which is to simply hunt down and shoot
>the person who let this happen in the first place (and downshift all
>user IDs), but there are laws against that :-).  And my customer seems
>quite adamant that I should support mixed case login IDs.

You just can't do it - the alias maps tend to use downcased checks
anyhow!

	Nigel.

