>makes them happier other than laziness from the part of the asministrator
>or plain tradition.
>
>   So in your case: just make an aliases file where every user is also
>defined with his/her lowercase username and redirected to the appropriate
>username.

I will probably alias the lower-case name to the appropriate mixed case
name one I get the original problem resolved.  In the mean time, trying
to do something like "kunkee3: Kunkee3" in the aliases file causes
sendmail to blow up.

>
>   And while you are at that, you might consider making other aesthetic
>aliases as well, such as including a dot or underscore between initials
>and name. If that's in the user data field of your passwd file, making
>a shell script to do it shoudln't be a big deal either.
>
>   Then face you boss and tell her/him you are giving everyone the
>commodity of being addressable as "JDoe", "jdoe", "J.Doe" or "J_Doe"
>at their convenience so that anyone can find them while they login
>with their beloved mixed case (or whatever) usernames.
>
>      Just my 2c.
>
>                                 jr

All good ideas.  Actually, I think a properly configured sendmail will
try to match names from the GCOS field if an login id is not matched.

Thanks to all for all the suggestions.  Too bad nothing has worked.
I think I have a brain-dead sendmail.  The obvious answer, ie. to 
add 'u' to the F= flags for the local mailer, does not work.  The
local mailer will handle something like "mail -d Kunkee3" correctly,
case-sensitive and all, so it is definitely sendmail, or some hidden
system/sendmail configuration of which I am unaware.


